 "source": {
  "0": {
   "long": false,
   "traits": {
    "Range": {
     "choice": "measured",
     "probs": {
      "measured": 0.95,
      "literature": 0.05
     },
     "said": [
      "Forests consisted primarily of coastal Douglas fir (Psuedotsuga menziesii), the dominant natural and commercial species in the PNW, with western hemlock ![Image block](doc:a05b363/tier:advanced/page:3/block:7) text_image Columbia River 2 6 7 4 WA OR Willamette 3 Valley 5 8 10 9 1 0 50 100 Km N Cascade Range A B C D E Fig.",
      "Carabid community metrics used in mixed-effects models, statistical method used to quantify them, range of values, model distribution, and sample size."
     ],
     "value": "measured"
    }
   }
  },
  "3": {
   "long": false,
   "traits": {
    "(no name, column 0)": {
     "said": [],
     "value": null
    },
    "Body length": {
     "choice": "measured",
     "probs": {
      "measured": 0.934,
      "literature": 0.066
     },
     "said": [
      "## Species trait data We quantified the functional niche occupied by each species based on three morphometric traits that reflect their food resources: body length, mandible length, and width between mandibles at their base (Deroulers and Bretagnolle 2019) and two traits that reflect their dispersal ability: rear leg length and wing type.",
      "Dendrogram and ordination were constructed from pairwise dissimilarity between each species based on body length, mandible length to width ratio, wing type, and leg length based on Gower’s distance."
     ],
     "value": "measured"
    },
    "Mandible ratio": {
     "choice": "measured",
     "probs": {
      "measured": 0.953,
      "literature": 0.047
     },
     "said": [
      "Dendrogram and ordination were constructed from pairwise dissimilarity between each species based on body length, mandible length to width ratio, wing type, and leg length based on Gower’s distance.",
      "For functional traits, average body length by species was 11 mm and mean mandible length to width ratio was 1.03 (Table 1)."
     ],
     "value": "measured"
    },
    "Dispersal PCoA†": {
     "choice": "measured",
     "probs": {
      "measured": 0.66,
      "literature": 0.34
     },
     "said": [
      "Lower PCoA values indicated species with high dispersal ability (i.e., developed wings but short legs), intermediate, positive values corresponded to species with low dispersal ability (i.e., no wings, short legs), and high positive values corresponded to moderate dispersers (i.e., no wings, long legs).",
      "We found that carabid communities present in the DS treatment were characterized by species with lower PCoA values and hence, better dispersal ability (Table 3)."
     ],
     "value": null
    }
   }
  },
